.\" Manual page for makedog.
.TH MAKEDOG 1 2026-07-23 "makedog" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
makedog \- a runner for server binaries: restart on builds, log every
run, send signals, run make targets, and more
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B makedog
.B watch
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIbinary-path\fR
.br
.B makedog
\fIrun#\fR|\fBlatest\fR[\fB~\fIN\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B makedog
.BR runs | next | tail
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B makedog
.B search
\fIregex\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B makedog
.BR status | restart | stop | start
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B makedog
.B signal
\fIname\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B makedog
supervises a server binary during development. It starts the binary,
watches the compiled file for changes, and restarts the process when a
new build lands. It deliberately does not watch source code or drive
recompilation; that is left to your editor, agent, or build system.
.PP
A \fIrun\fR is one execution of your binary, from start to
exit. And many runs are grouped into a \fIlineage\fR.
.PP
Your \fIproject\fR is your working directory. We anchor from here to
find binaries, read configs, and match runs & logs. A project can
have many binaries, each with its own lineage. A binary inside
the project is identified by its relative path, so \fIbin/server\fR,
\fI./bin/server\fR, or its full path, name one lineage.
.PP
Discovery favors convenience over exactness. Generally, an
unspecified run means the latest, and an unspecified binary means the
most recently active one. Also, a renamed or moved binary starts a
fresh lineage rather than erroring.
.PP
A bare path argument implies
.BR watch .
A bare run reference implies
.BR show .
For example, paths include slashes, like \fI./path\fR or \fIbin/name\fR.
Run references look like \fB33\fR, or
.BR latest [\fB~\fIN\fR].
.PP
Every run is recorded to a durable per-project store as JSONL, one
record per output line plus lifecycle events, and can be replayed,
tailed, searched, and diffed later. Older logs are compressed in the
background, and pruned per the retention policy (see
.B CONFIGURATION
below).
.PP
A running instance listens on a unix socket, so a second terminal can
query status, restart or stop the binary, or deliver signals remotely.
.PP
Each project can customize the signal menu. Taper the list and name
each signal (e.g. "reload config"). See
.B CONFIGURATION
below.
.SH VERBS
.TP
.BI watch " binary-path"
Supervise a binary (or script). Restarts it when the file's
modification time changes, logs the run, and accepts interactive
keypresses (see
.B INTERACTIVE KEYS
below).
.TP
\fIrun#\fR, \fBlatest\fR[\fB~\fIN\fR]
Replay a recorded run (longhand:
.B show
\fIref\fR).
.B latest~1
is the run before the latest, and so on.
.TP
.B runs
List this directory's recorded runs;
.B \-\-long
prints full metadata cards.
.TP
.B next
Wait for the next run to begin and stream it from its start.
.TP
.B tail
Follow live output across restarts, endlessly.
.TP
.BI search " regex"
Grep recorded runs (alias:
.BR grep );
exits 1 when nothing matches.
.TP
.BR info " [\fIref\fR]"
One run's metadata card, default latest;
.B \-\-json
emits one object.
.TP
.BR path " [\fIref\fR]"
Print a run log's file path; with no ref, the lineage directory.
.TP
.BR diff " [\fIa\fR [\fIb\fR]]"
Unified diff of two runs' output, default latest~1 vs latest.
.TP
.B status
List live makedog instances for the project.
.TP
.BR restart ", " stop ", " start
Command a live instance's binary remotely.
.TP
.BI signal " name"
Send the supervised binary a signal remotely, e.g. HUP or USR1.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Watch options
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " \fIpath\fR"
Path to a specific configuration file, rather than searching for one.
.SS Read options (show, next, tail)
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-follow
Keep streaming until the run exits (show).
.TP
.BI \-n " count"
Only the last \fIcount\fR records.
.TP
.BI \-\-since " age"
Only records within this age, like 30s or 5m (show).
.TP
.BI \-\-until " regex"
Stream until a line matches: exit 0 if matched, 1 if the run ended
first.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " duration"
Give up after this duration (e.g. 30s), exit 2.
.SS Runs options
.TP
.B \-\-long
Full metadata cards, as
.B info
shows.
.SS Search options
.TP
.BI \-\-runs " a..b"
Limit to a run number or range.
.TP
.BI \-\-since " age"
Only runs active within this age, like 2d or 6h.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-binaries
Search every binary recorded for the project.
.SS Control options (status, restart, stop, start, signal)
.TP
.BI \-\-instance " pid"
Target this makedog pid, when several are live. Mutating verbs require
it whenever more than one instance is running.
.SS Shared options (all but watch)
.TP
.BR \-\-json ", " \-\-jsonl
Emit raw JSONL records.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Strip ANSI styling.
.TP
.BI \-\-binary " path"
Which binary's runs, when several are recorded for the project.
.TP
.BI \-C " dir"
Operate on another project directory's runs.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.SH INTERACTIVE KEYS
While watching, makedog reads single keypresses from the terminal:
.TP
.B c
Clear the screen.
.TP
.B h
Show key help.
.TP
.B k
Write a mark into the run log.
.TP
.B m
Run make.
.TP
.B q
Quit makedog.
.TP
.B r
Restart the binary.
.TP
.B s
Choose a signal from a menu and send it to the binary.
.TP
.B t
Choose a make target from a menu and run it.
.TP
.B x
Start or stop the binary.
.SH CONFIGURATION
On
.BR watch ,
makedog loads
.B .makedog.toml
from the current directory (or the file named by
.BR \-\-config ).
A missing file is fine; a malformed one is ignored with a warning.
.SS [[signals]]
Configured signals replace the default
.B s
menu, which then offers only these, in config order. The remote
.B signal
verb is unaffected:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[[signals]]
signal = "USR1"
name   = "rotate logs"   # optional display name
.fi
.RE
.SS [logs]
Tunes run log retention. A watch-mode makedog runs one maintenance pass
shortly after startup, compressing idle runs with zstd and pruning by
count, age, and total size. The newest two runs are never touched.
Setting a field to zero disables that rule.
.TP
.B compress_after
Compress runs idle longer than this (default "24h").
.TP
.B keep_runs
Keep at most this many runs (default 500).
.TP
.B max_age
Delete runs idle longer than this, e.g. "90d" (default: never).
.TP
.B max_total
Delete oldest runs while the lineage exceeds this size, e.g. "512MB"
(default: unlimited).
.SH STOP SEMANTICS
However a stop arrives \(em a rebuild restart, the
.B r
or
.B x
keys, a remote
.BR stop " or " restart ,
or makedog itself exiting \(em the binary first receives SIGTERM and
two seconds to exit gracefully. An unresponsive process is then dealt
SIGKILL, noted in the log. Output produced during shutdown is drained
into the run log before it is sealed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B .makedog.toml
Per-project configuration, read from the working directory.
.TP
.B ~/.local/state/makedog
The run store. Each (working directory, binary path) pair owns a
lineage of numbered runs. Overridden by
.BR MAKEDOG_STATE_DIR .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MAKEDOG_STATE_DIR
Base directory of the run store, in place of ~/.local/state/makedog.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Honored when
.B MAKEDOG_STATE_DIR
is unset: the store lives in $XDG_STATE_HOME/makedog.
.TP
.B PAGER
Pager for replayed runs when stdout is a terminal (default "less
\-FRX").
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B search
exits 0 on a match, 1 when nothing matches. With
.BR \-\-until ,
readers exit 0 when a line matches, 1 when the run ends first, and 2 on
.B \-\-timeout
expiry. Other verbs exit 0 on success and nonzero on error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Supervise a server and restart it whenever it is rebuilt:
.PP
.RS
.nf
makedog bin/server
.fi
.RE
.PP
Replay the previous run, plainly, through the pager:
.PP
.RS
.nf
makedog latest~1 \-\-plain
.fi
.RE
.PP
Block until the server reports readiness, or give up after 30 seconds:
.PP
.RS
.nf
makedog next \-\-until 'listening on' \-\-timeout 30s
.fi
.RE
.PP
Search the last two days of runs for errors:
.PP
.RS
.nf
makedog search 'ERROR|panic' \-\-since 2d
.fi
.RE
.PP
Tell the running server to reload its config, from another terminal:
.PP
.RS
.nf
makedog signal HUP
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR make (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR less (1)
